     "id": "139",
     "word": "aŋgōñcʰa",
     "gloss": "towel",
     "native": null,
     "phonemic": null,
     "notes": "",
     "etymology": "<number>139</number> <b>aṅgōñcha</b> m., <i>aṅgōñchana</i>- n. 'towel' lex. [Poss. <smallcaps><a data-entry=\"114\">áṅga-¹</a>, uñchá</smallcaps>- m. 'gleaning esp. of single grains' (for   meaning 'sweeping together, wiping' cf. development of meaning under <smallcaps><a data-entry=\"1680\">úñchati</a></smallcaps> and <smallcaps><a data-entry=\"9011\">prōñchati</a></smallcaps>). But N. Bi. H. point to MIA. <i>*aṅga-u{ñ}cha</i>- (with <i><a data-entry=\"139\">aṅgōñcha</a></i>- as late Sanskritization), poss. a MIA. form with *-<i>vuñcha</i>- anal. replacing <i>puñcha</i>- &lt; <i>prōñcha</i>- (as suggested by Platts UD s.v. and P. Tedesco JAOS 77. 195). In either case loss of second nasal, through dissimilation, was early (contrast <smallcaps><a data-entry=\"1680\">úñchati</a>, <a data-entry=\"9011\">prōñchati</a></smallcaps>). In any case Bloch LM 286 wrongly &lt; <i>aṅgavastra</i>.]",
